:gobekli-tepe-incised-female a :human-figure ;
   rdfs:label "Incised Female Figure from the “Lion Pillar Building”, Göbekli Tepe" ;
   :described-by <https://doi.org/10.1515/pz-2025-2049> ,
      <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/G%C3%B6bekli_Tepe> ;
   :earliest-date -8200 ;
   :latest-date -7800 ;
   :material-note "Limestone" ;
   :apparent-gender :female ;
   :note "From Zimmermann, “The Raging Male”, Praehistorische Zeitschrift 2025, n. 34: “This observation includes eventually also the rather popular engraving on a stone slab of an alleged naked ‘female’ from the ‘Lion Pillar Building’ (Schmidt 2007, 290 Fig. 104) (Fig. 6), spreading her legs either for being in labour (Bodet 2021, 147–149), or being forcefully penetrated by a penis belonging to an otherwise invisible male (Hodder/Meskell 2011, 239). Çiğdem Atakuman (Middle East Technical University, Ankara) alerted me of an entirely different possible reading of this figure, since neither the slim ‘breasts’ nor the rather slender torso appears to belong to a (pregnant) woman. The ‘breasts’ could therefore depict the arms of another (real or imagined) individual acting from behind, changing the narrative altogether (personal communication).”" ;
   :in-modern-country-note "Turkey" ;
   :representative-latlong-point (37.223333 38.922500) ;
   :wikimedia-commons-image-page <https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Urfa_museum_Woman_figure_Göbekli_Tepe_-_Neolithic_age_4750.jpg> .

:tell-mureybet-female-figurine a :human-figure ;
   rdfs:label "Tell Mureybit Female Figurine" ;
   :described-by <https://virtual-museum-syria.org/aleppo/female-figurine-2/> ;
   :approximate-date -8000 ;
   :material-note "Ceramic" ;
   :note "From Virtual Museum Syria website: “Most of the female figurines made during the Neolithic period were shaped out of clay that was slightly hardened by fire but not really baked. This figurine was one of eight discovered on the site of Tell Mureybet. It is clear that the artist sought to emphasize the part of the body linked to reproduction – the pelvic region, or maternal womb, and the breasts, or nourishing bosom. It is significant that all the figurines of this type lack facial features, since these were not directly related to the intended symbolism.”" ;
   :in-modern-country-note "Syria" ;
   :representative-latlong-point (36.0434 38.1287) ;
   :art-historical-culture-or-tradition :pre-pottery-neolithic ;
   :thumbnail-image <https://virtual-museum-syria.org/aleppo/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2021/06/fINbasiC-2-800x1574.jpg> .

