:male-torso-portonaccio-veii a :human-figure ;
   rdfs:label "Male Torso, Possibly Hercules, Portonaccio Sanctuary, Veii" ;
   :described-by <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Portonaccio> ,
      <https://elearning.uniroma1.it/pluginfile.php/1375455/mod_resource/content/1/AnnFaina%20XXI%202014%20Carlucci-Michetti%20Portonaccio.pdf> ;
   :approximate-date -550 ;
   :in-modern-country-note "Italy" ;
   :material-note "Terracotta" ;
   :apparent-gender :male ;
   :note "Translated from article (Carlucci and Michetti, Annali Faina XXI, 2014, p. 509): “In this phase, in the sacred area, still limited to the eastern sector, the large male torso is dedicated, an exceptional votive offering, perhaps depicting Hercules (Fig. 25), the first of the rich gallery of terracotta sculptures ... which to date represents the largest number of original terracotta works recovered from an Etruscan sanctuary between the Archaic and Classical periods. It is the first example of a votive statue on a larger-than-life scale ...” The article dates the statue to the mid-6th century BCE." ;
   :representative-latlong-point (42.021111 12.390833) ; # Portonaccio sanctuary, Veii, from Wikipedia
   :wikimedia-commons-image-page <https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Male_torso_Veii.jpg> .

:phrasikleia-kore a :human-figure ;
   rdfs:label "Phrasikleia Kore" ;
   :described-by <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phrasikleia_Kore> ;
   :earliest-date -550 ;
   :latest-date -540 ;
   :in-modern-country-note "Greece" ;
   :material-note "Parian marble" ;
   :height 1.79 ;
   :apparent-gender :female ;
   :note "From Wikipedia: “The Phrasikleia Kore is an Archaic Greek funerary statue by the artist Aristion of Paros, created between 550 and 540 BCE. It was found carefully buried in the ancient city of Myrrhinous (modern Merenta) in Attica and excavated in 1972. The exceptional preservation of the statue and the intact nature of the polychromy elements makes the Phrasikleia Kore one of the most important works of Archaic art. ... The statue is made of Parian marble, and stands 179 centimeters (6.92 ft) high; it rises on a pedestal that gives a total height of 211 centimeters (7 ft) high.”" ;
   :representative-latlong-point (37.8549039025 23.9483773191) ; # TO CONFIRM: source unknown (Wikipedia's Myrrhinus coord is 37.872812 23.9678945)
   :wikimedia-commons-image-page <https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aristone_di_paros,_kore_per_la_tomba_di_phrasikleia,_da_merenda_(myrrhinous)_in_attica,_550-540_ac_ca._01,_n._4889.jpg> .

:kore-lyons a :human-figure ;
   rdfs:label "Kore of Lyons" ;
   :described-by <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kore_of_Lyons> ;
   :earliest-date -550 ;
   :latest-date -540 ;
   :in-modern-country-note "Greece" ;
   :material-note "Pentelic marble" ;
   :height 0.64 ;
   :apparent-gender :female ;
   :note "From Wikipedia: “The Kore of Lyons is a Greek statue of Pentelic marble depicting a bust of a young girl of the kore type, conserved at the musée des beaux-arts de Lyon, France. Deriving from the Athenian Acropolis, it is generally dated to the 540s BC. ... It was one of the numerous Korai of the Acropolis of Athens discovered during the construction of the old Acropolis Museum in the Perserschutt, a layer of destruction from the Persian Wars, containing material from the 570s BC down to the end of the sixth century BC. It represents the first example of the Ionian influence which entered Attic sculpture in the second half of the century and first use of Ionian costume in Attica.”" ;
   :representative-latlong-point (37.97166667 23.72611111) ; # Acropolis of Athens, from Wikipedia
   :wikimedia-commons-image-page <https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lyons_Kore_MBA_Lyon_H1993.jpg> .

:kroisos-kouros a :human-figure ;
   rdfs:label "Kroisos Kouros" ;
   :described-by <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kroisos_Kouros> ;
   :earliest-date -540 ;
   :latest-date -515 ;
   :material-note "Marble" ;
   :height 1.95 ;
   :apparent-gender :male ;
   :in-modern-country-note "Greece" ;
   :note "From Wikipedia: “The Kroisos Kouros is a marble kouros from Anavyssos in Attica which functioned as a grave marker for a fallen young warrior named Kroisos. ... The sculpture is dated to the Late Archaic Period c. 540–515 BC.” The base inscription reads: “Stop and show pity beside the marker of Kroisos, dead, whom, when he was in the front ranks, raging Ares destroyed.” Illegally excavated at Anavyssos, identified in Paris in 1937, and now in the National Archaeological Museum, Athens (inv. 3851)." ;
   :representative-latlong-point (37.733333 23.95) ; # ll for Anavyssos, from Wikipedia
   :wikimedia-commons-image-page <https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kouros_anavissos.jpg> .

:merenda-kouros a :human-figure ;
   rdfs:label "Merenda Kouros" ;
   :described-by <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Merenda_Kouros> ;
   :earliest-date -540 ;
   :latest-date -530 ;
   :in-modern-country-note "Greece" ;
   :material-note "Parian marble" ;
   :height 1.89 ;
   :apparent-gender :male ;
   :note "From Wikipedia: “The Merenda Kouros (NAMA 4890) is a Late Archaic Greek Kouros, created approximately 540-530 B.C., measuring 1.89 meters tall and made of Parian marble. ... Extremely well preserved, and maintaining some traces of pigment. It was discovered alongside the Phrasikleia Kore in present-day Merenda, Greece in 1972, 25 miles Southeast of Athens. The area was once a part of the necropolis of the Attic Deme of Myrrhinus.”" ;
   :representative-latlong-point (37.8549039025 23.9483773191) ; # TO CONFIRM: source unknown (Wikipedia's Myrrhinus coord is 37.872812 23.9678945)
   :wikimedia-commons-image-page <https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kouros,_da_merernda_(myrrhinous)_in_attica,_540-530_ac_ca._01,_n._4890.jpg> .

:peplos-kore a :human-figure ;
   rdfs:label "Peplos Kore" ;
   :described-by <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peplos_Kore> ,
      <https://www.theacropolismuseum.gr/en/statue-kore-peplos-kore> ;
   :approximate-date -530 ;
   :in-modern-country-note "Greece" ;
   :material-note "Parian marble" ;
   :height 1.18 ;
   :apparent-gender :female ;
   :note "From Wikipedia: “The Peplos Kore is an ancient sculpture from the Acropolis of Athens. ... Although this statue is one of the most famous examples of a kore, it is actually not considered a typical one. The statue is not completely straight, her face is leaned slightly to the side, and she is standing with her weight shifted to one leg. The other part of the statue's name, peplos, is based on the popular archaic Greek gown for women. ... The Peplos Kore is ascribed to the Rampin Master, who is named for another head, very similar in style, which was in the Rampin Collection and is now on display in the Louvre.”" ;
   :representative-latlong-point (37.97166667 23.72611111) ; # Acropolis of Athens (find-spot, north-west of the Erechtheion), from Wikipedia
   :wikimedia-commons-image-page <https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ACMA_679_Kore_1.JPG> ;
   :thumbnail-image <https://www.theacropolismuseum.gr/sites/default/files/exhibits_images/7782_1.jpg> ,
      <https://www.theacropolismuseum.gr/sites/default/files/exhibits_images/7782_2.jpg> ,
      <https://www.theacropolismuseum.gr/sites/default/files/exhibits_images/7782_3.jpg> ,
      <https://www.theacropolismuseum.gr/sites/default/files/exhibits_images/7782_5.jpg> .

:pozo-moro-mausoleum a :group-of-human-figures ;
   rdfs:label "Friezes on Mausoleum of Pozo Moro" ;
   :described-by <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mausoleum_of_Pozo_Moro> ;
   :earliest-date -525 ;
   :latest-date -490 ;
   :material-note "Stone" ;
   :note "Summarized from Wikipedia: Iberian tower-tomb (c. 10 m high, on a three-level square pedestal 3.65 m wide), with four corner lions and relief panels including a banquet served by a two-headed monster, a hero confronting beasts, and other narrative scenes strongly influenced by Hittite and Syrian iconography. Found in 1970 near Chinchilla de Monte-Aragón (Albacete) and reassembled at the Museo Arqueológico Nacional, Madrid." ;
   :in-modern-country-note "Spain" ;
   :representative-latlong-point (38.836389 -1.696667) ;
   :wikimedia-commons-image-page <https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Madrid_Pozo_Moro_03.JPG> .

:yue-kneeling-figure a :human-figure ;
   rdfs:label "Yue Kneeling Figure" ;
   :described-by <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baiyue> ,
      <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yue_(state)> ;
   :earliest-date -525 ;
   :latest-date -333 ;
   :note "Date and mapped point approximate." ;
   :in-modern-country-note "China" ;
