:plain-of-jars-frogman a :human-figure ;
   rdfs:label "Plain of Jars Frogman Carving" ;
   :described-by <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plain_of_Jars> ,
      <https://www.waymarking.com/waymarks/WMF2MY_The_Frogman_Plain_of_JarsPhonsavan_City_Xiengkhouang_Province_Laos> ;
   :earliest-date -500 ;
   :latest-date 500 ;
   :in-modern-country-note "Laos" ;
   :material-note "Sandstone" ;
   :note "From Wikipedia: “Of the jars, only one jar has been found with iconography carved into it. The stone jars are undecorated, with the exception of a single jar at Site 1. This jar has a human ‘frogman’ bas-relief carved on the exterior.” Parallels have been drawn with the rock paintings at Huashan in Guangxi, China (ca. 500 BCE – 200 CE)." ;
   :representative-latlong-point (19.430639 103.154256) ;
   :thumbnail-image <https://img.geocaching.com/waymarking/large/ffdf15d2-4d98-475c-a241-b4b3c158b08a.jpg> .

:aphaia-east-pediment a :group-of-human-figures ;
   rdfs:label "East Pediment of the Temple of Aphaia, Aegina" ;
   :described-by <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Temple_of_Aphaia#Eastern_pediment> ,
      <https://smarthistory.org/east-and-west-pediments-from-the-temple-of-aphaia-aegina/> ;
   :earliest-date -490 ;
   :latest-date -480 ;
   :in-modern-country-note "Greece" ;
   :material-note "Parian marble" ;
   :note "From Wikipedia: “The east pediment depicts the first Trojan War, not the one described by Homer but the war of Heracles against the king of Troy Laomedon, with Telamon figuring prominently as he fights alongside Heracles against king Laomedon. The Goddess stands in the center of the gable, overseeing the battle that rages on either side. …This pediment is thought to be later than the west pediment and shows a number of features appropriate to the Classical period: the statues show a dynamic posture especially in the case of Athena, chiastic composition, and intricate filling of the space using the legs of fallen combatants to fill the difficult decreasing angles of the pediment.”" ;
   :representative-latlong-point (37.754722 23.533333) ; # Temple of Aphaia, Aegina (find-spot)
   :art-historical-culture-or-tradition :archaic-greece ;
   :wikimedia-commons-image-page <https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_east_pediment_of_the_Temple_of_Aphaia,_ca._505–500_BC,_Glyptothek,_Munich_(41606793432).jpg> .

