:ekven-cemetery-crouched-figure a :human-figure ;
   rdfs:label "Ekven Cemetery Crouched Figure" ;
   :described-by <https://www.google.com/books/edition/Problems_of_Ethnic_History_in_the_Bering/pckRnk29-YoC?hl=en&gbpv=0> ,
      <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ekven> ;
   :earliest-date -200 ;
   :latest-date 500 ;
   :material-note "Walrus tusk" ;
   :note "From Arutiunov and Dorian A. Sergeev 1975, trans., p. 140: “In a sculptured figurine, the above-mentioned half-closed eye motif is encountered (Fig. 54). One can see the realism and precise depiction of the natural form and proportions of the human body in the dynamic and expressive figurine. Conventionally, disproportion in the depiction of the human body was more common in Eskimo art. Judging by the hole carved in the groin area of the torso, the figurine must have been the end piece for a staff or baton. The slot in the back area must have been used for attaching additional ornaments made of soft material, such as fur.”" ;
   :in-modern-country-note "Russia" ;
   :representative-latlong-point (66.025833 -170.073333) . # Ekven Burial Ground, ~30 km south of Uelen on Cape Dezhnev; coordinates from UNESCO tentative-list dossier

:dotaku-kagawa a :group-of-human-figures ;
   rdfs:label "Dōtaku bell (reportedly from Kagawa)" ;
   :described-by <https://colbase.nich.go.jp/collection_items/tnm/J-37433?locale=en> ,
      <https://www.tnm.jp/modules/r_collection/index.php?controller=dtl&colid=J37433&t=now> ,
      <https://emuseum.nich.go.jp/detail?langId=ja&webView=&content_base_id=100201&content_part_id=0&content_pict_id=0> ;
   :earliest-date -200 ;
   :latest-date -1 ;
   :apparent-gender :male, :female ;
   :height 0.427 ;
   :material-note "Bronze" ;
   :note "From Tokyo National Museum: “Around 60 of these are decorated with pictorial representations. This dotaku is particularly renowned for its decorativeness and it was the first dotaku to be designated a national treasure. Motifs include men hunting deer and wild boar, for instance, or women pounding a mortar with a pestle. These offer unique insights into life at the time. … Note also how the heads of the human figures are depicted in shapes of circles and triangles. The former shape represents men and the latter women.”" ;
   :in-modern-country-note "Japan" ;
   :representative-latlong-point (34.266667 133.95) ; # Kagawa Prefecture (Wikipedia); find-spot only "reportedly Kagawa"
   :wikimedia-commons-image-page <https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:J-37433_E0102666.jpg> , # two women pounding a mortar
      <https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:J-37433_E0102652.jpg> , # whole bell
      <https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:J-37433_E0102670.jpg> , # archer shooting a deer
      <https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:J-37433_E0102665.jpg> , # archer, animals
      <https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:J-37433_E0102667.jpg> . # figure holding I-shaped tool

