   :earliest-date 550 ;
   :latest-date 600 ;
   :in-modern-country-note "Japan" ;
   :material-note "Red, black and green pigment on granite" ;
   :apparent-gender :unclear ;
   :note "From article: “Figure 4. Visual synthesis of core findings: ... b) strategic positioning within specific architectural elements here on the rear wall of Tashiroōta tomb (adapted from the original work by Kusaka Hakkō; © courtesy of the National Museum of Japanese History) ...” ... From Wikipedia: “The 2.3 meter wide and 1.1 meter high waist stone on the back wall of the burial chamber and the wall on the right side of the middle chamber are decorated with continuous triangular patterns, using the three colors of red, black, and green on the granite rock surface. The drawings also depict people, ships, shields, takatsuki (tall cups), bracken (warabi) patterns, concentric circles, and flowers. ... it is estimated that the tumulus was built in the latter half of the 6th century.”" ;
   :representative-latlong-point (33.394806 130.515167) ; # Tashiroōta Kofun, from Wikipedia
   :thumbnail-image <https://static.cambridge.org/binary/version/id/urn:cambridge.org:id:binary:20260916130530288-0360:S0003598X26103779:S0003598X26103779_fig4.png> .

