:man-mound-sauk-county a :human-figure ;
   rdfs:label "Man Mound" ;
   :described-by <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Man_Mound> ,
      <https://wisconsinfirstnations.org/man-mound-county-park/> ;
   :earliest-date 750 ;
   :latest-date 1200 ;
   :material-note "Earth (raised earthwork)" ;
   :note "From Wikipedia: “The Man Mound is a precontact earthwork of a humanoid figure located in Greenfield, Sauk County, Wisconsin, east of the city of Baraboo. Constructed during the Late Woodland period, the mound is the only surviving anthropomorphic effigy mound in North America. The mound depicts a humanoid figure with horns or a horned headdress and may have held religious or ceremonial significance to its builders …When it was built, the figure was 214 feet (65 m) long and raised 2.5 feet (0.76 m) from the ground. The legs and feet of the figure were destroyed by the construction of Man Mound Road and a cow pasture across the road, leaving the modern mound roughly 50 feet (15 m) shorter than its original length.”" ;
   :in-modern-country-note "United States" ;
   :representative-latlong-point (43.48861 -89.67083) ;
   :thumbnail-image <https://wisconsinfirstnations.org/wp-content/uploads/2018/10/ManMound.jpg> ;
   :wikimedia-commons-image-page <https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Man_Mound.jpg> .

:shaka-birth-todaiji a :group-of-human-figures ;
   rdfs:label "Shaka at Birth (Tōdai-ji)" ;
   :described-by <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shaka_at_Birth_(T%C5%8Ddai-ji)> ;
   :earliest-date 750 ;
   :latest-date 759 ;
   :in-modern-country-note "Japan" ;
   :material-note "Gilt bronze" ;
   :height 0.475 ;
   :apparent-gender :male ;
   :note "From Wikipedia: “The statue and its basin date to the Nara period (710–794) ... This example at Tōdai-ji is generally dated to the 750s. ... Evoking the legend of his birth, the statue of the infant Buddha has its right arm raised, pointing to heaven, and left arm pendant, pointing to the earth; he is bare chested and wears a skirt. ... The exterior has incised images of human figures, animals real and imaginary, birds, and butterflies, set against a landscape of flowers, grasses, shrubs, trees, mountain peaks, clouds, and pagodas.”" ;
   :representative-latlong-point (34.68916667 135.83972222) ; # Tōdai-ji, Nara, from Wikipedia
   :wikimedia-commons-image-page <https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shaka_at_birth_basin.JPG> .

:bell-king-seongdeok a :group-of-human-figures ;
   rdfs:label "Bell of King Seongdeok" ;
   :described-by <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bell_of_King_Seongdeok> ;
   :approximate-date 771 ;
   :material-note "Bronze" ;
   :note "From Wikipedia: “There are many relief patterns on the bell, including flower patterns along the rim and shoulder. There are also reliefs of lotus flowers, grass, and a pair of two apsaras (heavenly maidens). The striking point of the bell (dangjwa) is also in the shape of a lotus and sits between two of the apsara reliefs. The bottom of the bell is in a rhombic shape, designed to augment the sound reverberation.”" ;
   :in-modern-country-note "Korea" ;
   :representative-latlong-point ( 35.829575 129.227928 ) ;
   :wikimedia-commons-image-page <https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Apsara_relief._Bell_of_King_Seongdeok.National_Museum_of_Gyeongju.Korea.2018.P1410600.jpg> .

:yakushi-nyorai-jingoji a :human-figure ;
   rdfs:label "Yakushi Nyorai (Jingo-ji)" ;
   :described-by <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Statue_of_Yakushi_Nyorai_(Jingo-ji)> ;
   :earliest-date 775 ;
   :latest-date 825 ;
   :in-modern-country-note "Japan" ;
   :material-note "Hinoki (Japanese cypress), light pigment" ;
   :height 1.697 ;
   :apparent-gender :male ;
   :note "From Wikipedia: “The Statue of Yakushi Nyorai (Japanese: 木造薬師如来立像, Hepburn: Mokuzō Yakushi Nyorai Ritsuzō) is a late 8th to early 9th-century Japanese Buddhist sculpture dating to the early Heian period depicting the standing figure of Bhaisajyaguru, or the Medicine Buddha. Designated a National Treasure of Japan, the Yakushi Nyorai serves as the principal figure of worship at Jingo-ji, Ukyō-ku, Kyoto. It is deemed a significant masterpiece of early Heian art, as well as a major icon of Shingon Buddhist history. ... The Yakushi Nyorai statue is 169.7 centimetres (5 ft 7 in) tall and is carved from a single block of hinoki cypress wood.”" ;
   :representative-latlong-point (35.05501667 135.67086667) ; # Jingo-ji, Kyoto, from Wikipedia
   :wikimedia-commons-image-page <https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Domon_Ken_-_Yakushi_Nyorai_(Bhaisajyaguru)_Jingo-ji.jpg> ,
      <https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yakushi_Nyorai_Jingoji2.JPG> .

