      <https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Braccio_del_colosso_di_Costantino_I_ai_Musei_Capitolini.jpg> ,
      <https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Colossal_hand,_Roman_-_Musei_Capitolini_-_Rome,_Italy_-_DSC06255.jpg> ,
      <https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Colossal_statue_of_Constantine_-_Palazzo_dei_Conservatori_-_Musei_Capitolini_-_Rome_2016.JPG_(3).jpg> ,
      <https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Statua_colossale_di_Costantino_I.jpg> .

:missorium-of-theodosius-i a :group-of-human-figures ;
   rdfs:label "Missorium of Theodosius I" ;
   :described-by <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Missorium_of_Theodosius_I> ;
   :approximate-date 388 ;
   :in-modern-country-note "Spain" ;
   :material-note "Silver" ;
   :apparent-gender :male , :female ;
   :note "From Wikipedia: “The dish shows a hieratic image of the Emperor framed in an arcade, giving a document contained in a diptych, possibly a letter of appointment, to a much smaller senior official, flanked by his two co-emperors, Valentinian II and his own son Arcadius, and bodyguards who can be identified by their hairstyles, torcs and shields as belonging to the German imperial bodyguard.” ... “The putti fly above a reclining figure of Tellus or Terra, the Roman earth goddess.” ... “The missorium comes from a treasure of silver objects that also included two plain silver cups, now missing, discovered by a labourer in 1847 in Almendralejo, close to Mérida in the Spanish province of Badajoz.”" ;
   :representative-latlong-point (38.68 -6.41) ; # Almendralejo, Badajoz (find-spot)
   :wikimedia-commons-image-page <https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Disc_of_Theodosius.png> .

:statuette-god-teisheba a :human-figure ;
   rdfs:label "Statuette of God Teisheba" ;
   :described-by <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Statuette_of_God_Teisheba> ;
   :earliest-date -800 ;
   :latest-date -700 ;
   :in-modern-country-note "Armenia" ;
   :material-note "Bronze" ;
   :apparent-gender :male ;
   :note "From Wikipedia: “The Statuette of God Teisheba is an Urartian (Kingdom of Van) bronze statuette made in the 8-7th century BC, found near Teishebaini (Karmir Blur) in 1941, depicting the Araratian (Urartian) god of storms and thunder Teisheba. ... The statuette looks like a young man who in full height stands on a foliate base. The man wears a gown on which there are characteristic ornaments of Urartian period: a quadrangle with a rosette in the centre, a belt on his waist, a fringed band over his shoulder.”" ;
   :representative-latlong-point (40.153888 44.453366) ; # Teishebaini (Karmir Blur)
   :wikimedia-commons-image-page <https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tesheiba02~.jpg> .

:baalshillem-temple-boy a :human-figure ;
   rdfs:label "Baalshillem Temple Boy" ;
   :described-by <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baalshillem_Temple_Boy> ;
   :earliest-date -425 ;
   :latest-date -400 ;
   :in-modern-country-note "Lebanon" ;
   :material-note "Marble" ;
   :height 0.35 ;
   :apparent-gender :male ;
   :note "From Wikipedia: “The Baalshillem Temple Boy, or Ba'al Sillem Temple Boy, is a votive statue of a ‘temple boy’ with a Phoenician inscription known as KAI 281. It was found along with a number of other votive statues of children near the canal in the Temple of Eshmun in 1963-64 by Maurice Dunand, and is currently in the National Museum of Beirut. ... The inscription is dated from the end of the 5th century BCE.”" ;
   :representative-latlong-point (33.585556 35.398056) ; # Temple of Eshmun
   :wikimedia-commons-image-page <https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Votive_statue_from_eshmun.jpg> .

:bronze-votive-figure-pizzidimonte a :human-figure ;
   rdfs:label "Bronze Votive Figure from Pizzidimonte" ;
   :described-by <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bronze_votive_figure_from_Pizzidimonte> ;
   :earliest-date -500 ;
   :latest-date -480 ;
   :in-modern-country-note "Italy" ;
   :material-note "Bronze" ;
   :height 0.232 ;
   :apparent-gender :male ;
   :note "From Wikipedia: “This bronze votive statuette shows a young man wearing a bordered tunic with pointed boots. The figure is still fixed to its original lead mount. The small sculpture stands upright with drapery closely wrapped around his body, right hand extended forward with the left hand placed firmly on his waist. ... Though nothing is known about the exact findspot of the bronze figurine, the important Etruscan settlement at Gonfienti was discovered in 1996 only one kilometre away from Pizzidimonte and it is therefore likely that it was originally found there.”" ;
   :representative-latlong-point (43.860556 11.125833) ; # Gonfienti (Wikipedia coord), probable find-spot near Pizzidimonte
   :wikimedia-commons-image-page <https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Votive_figure_from_Pizzidimonte,_Prato_(1).jpg> .

:casa-nocera-statue-a a :human-figure ;
   rdfs:label "Statue A from the Casa Nocera Necropolis" ;
   :described-by <https://it.wikipedia.org/wiki/Necropoli_di_Casa_Nocera> ;
   :earliest-date -700 ;
   :latest-date -675 ;
   :in-modern-country-note "Italy" ;
   :material-note "Shelly limestone" ;
   :height 0.745 ;
   :apparent-gender :unclear ;
   :note "Translated from Italian Wikipedia: “The two statues in shelly limestone, datable to the first decades of the 7th century BC, were found reused on the covering of tomb C ... Both lack the lower part of the legs, since they were fitted with long tenons that allowed them to be fixed in the ground. The standing figures wear a triangular loincloth incised with geometric motifs and a wide belt; statue A (preserved height 74.5 cm) wears its hair in a long braid and raises its forearms with the hands joined behind the nape ... The different hairstyles led Friedrich-Wilhelm von Hase to suggest that statue B depicts a man and statue A a woman, a hypothesis Maggiani judged suggestive but not adequately demonstrated.”" ;
   :representative-latlong-point (43.2975 10.616667) ; # Casale Marittimo; necropolis just outside the town
   :wikimedia-commons-image-page <https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Figure_da_necropoli_di_casa_nocera,_casale_marittimo,_700-680_a.c._circa,_statua_A.JPG> .

:casa-nocera-statue-b a :human-figure ;
   rdfs:label "Statue B from the Casa Nocera Necropolis" ;
   :described-by <https://it.wikipedia.org/wiki/Necropoli_di_Casa_Nocera> ;
   :earliest-date -700 ;
   :latest-date -675 ;
   :in-modern-country-note "Italy" ;
   :material-note "Shelly limestone" ;
   :height 0.85 ;
   :apparent-gender :unclear ;
   :note "Translated from Italian Wikipedia: “The two statues in shelly limestone, datable to the first decades of the 7th century BC, were found reused on the covering of tomb C ... Both lack the lower part of the legs, since they were fitted with long tenons that allowed them to be fixed in the ground. The standing figures wear a triangular loincloth incised with geometric motifs and a wide belt; ... statue B (85 cm) has a short tail on the shoulders, the left hand lowered to the belly and the right drawn to the chest, and preserves a fragment of the right eye, worked in black stone and inset in the eye socket. ... The different hairstyles led Friedrich-Wilhelm von Hase to suggest that statue B depicts a man and statue A a woman, a hypothesis Maggiani judged suggestive but not adequately demonstrated.”" ;
   :representative-latlong-point (43.2975 10.616667) ; # Casale Marittimo; necropolis just outside the town
   :wikimedia-commons-image-page <https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Figure_da_necropoli_di_casa_nocera,_casale_marittimo,_700-680_a.c._circa,_statua_B.JPG> .

:orans-of-kyiv a :human-figure ;
   rdfs:label "Orans of Kyiv" ;
   :described-by <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orans_of_Kyiv> ,
      <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Sophia_Cathedral,_Kyiv> ;
   :earliest-date 1011 ;
   :latest-date 1037 ;
   :in-modern-country-note "Ukraine" ;
   :material-note "Mosaic" ;
   :height 6 ;
   :apparent-gender :female ;
   :note "From Wikipedia: “The Virgin Orans, Oranta (The Great Panagia) is a well-known Orthodox Christian depiction of the Virgin Mary in prayer with extended arms. The 6-meter-high mosaic is located in the vault of the chancel of Saint Sophia Cathedral in Kyiv in Ukraine. The icon has been present in the cathedral since its foundation by Yaroslav I the Wise in the 11th century. The Virgin's solemn and static posture, the characteristic folds of her garments, and her pensive expression indicate the design was strongly influenced by Byzantine art.”" ;
   :representative-latlong-point (50.4528 30.5144) ; # Saint Sophia Cathedral, Kyiv
   :wikimedia-commons-image-page <https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Oranta-Kyiv.jpg> .

:virgin-hodegetria-torcello a :group-of-human-figures ;
   rdfs:label "Virgin Hodegetria Apse Mosaic, Torcello Cathedral" ;
   :described-by <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Torcello_Cathedral> ;
   :earliest-date 1075 ;
   :latest-date 1200 ;
   :in-modern-country-note "Italy" ;
   :material-note "Mosaic" ;
   :apparent-gender :female , :male ;
   :note "From Wikipedia: “The main apse has an 11th-century mosaic of famous beauty of the standing Virgin Hodegetria, isolated against a huge gold background, above a register of standing saints. These seem originally late 11th-century, by a team of Byzantine mosaicists, but the main figure was reworked a century later after an earthquake, while the saints remain from the first period of work.”" ;
   :representative-latlong-point (45.4982 12.4193) ; # Torcello Cathedral
   :wikimedia-commons-image-page <https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Torcello_-_Santa_Maria_Assunta_-_mosaics_of_the_choir.JPG> .

:virgin-child-apse-mosaic-gelati a :group-of-human-figures ;
   rdfs:label "Virgin and Child Apse Mosaic, Gelati Monastery" ;
   :described-by <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gelati_Monastery> ;
   :earliest-date 1106 ;
   :latest-date 1200 ;
   :in-modern-country-note "Georgia" ;
   :material-note "Mosaic" ;
   :apparent-gender :female , :male ;
   :note "From Wikipedia: “The interiors of the monastery hold mosaics in classic Byzantine style illustrating aspects of Christian belief. The largest, a 12th-century masterpiece depicting the Virgin Mary holding the baby Jesus, dominates the apse of the main church, and is an artwork of cultural importance in Georgia.”" ;
   :representative-latlong-point (42.294722 42.768056) ; # Gelati Monastery
   :wikimedia-commons-image-page <https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sanctuary_apse_mosaics_(12th_century)_and_murals_(16th_century)._Gelati_Monastery.jpg> .

:golden-madonna-essen a :group-of-human-figures ;
   rdfs:label "Golden Madonna of Essen" ;
   :described-by <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Golden_Madonna_of_Essen> ;
   :approximate-date 980 ;
   :in-modern-country-note "Germany" ;
   :material-note "Wood (probably poplar) covered with gold leaf; cloisonné enamel eyes" ;
   :height 0.74 ;
   :apparent-gender :female , :male ;
   :note "From Wikipedia: “The image is a wooden core covered with sheets of thin gold leaf and is part of the treasury of Essen Cathedral in Germany. ... Dated around the year 980 A.D., it is both the oldest known sculpture of the Madonna and the oldest free-standing medieval sculpture north of the Alps ... Mary is depicted sitting on a stool, with a slightly oversized Christ child figure sitting on her lap.”" ;
   :representative-latlong-point (51.455833 7.013611) ; # Essen Cathedral (former abbey church)
   :wikimedia-commons-image-page <https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Goldene-Madonna-Essen.jpg> .

:hadrian-tel-shalem a :group-of-human-figures ;
   rdfs:label "Bronze Statue of Hadrian from Tel Shalem" ;
   :described-by <https://www.judaism-and-rome.org/bronze-statue-hadrian-tel-shalem-135-137-ce> ,
      <https://followinghadrian.com/2014/10/28/bronze-statue-of-hadrian-from-the-legionary-camp-at-tel-shalem-judaea-israel-museum/> ;
   :earliest-date 132 ;
   :latest-date 137 ;
   :in-modern-country-note "Israel" ;
   :material-note "Bronze" ;
   :apparent-gender :male ;
   :note "From Following Hadrian: “The statue likely portrays Hadrian in the pose of the supreme military commander greeting his troops (adlocutio) or as a conqueror stepping on a defeated enemy (a head of a youth was found next to the statue). However, it is uncertain whether the head and the armour originally belonged together.” ... From Judaism and Rome: “The head and arm are attached to a cuirassed torso, which is decorated with a relief depicting a battle scene with a group of three pairs of soldiers who fight in duels.”" ;
   :representative-latlong-point (32.399263 35.526037) ; # Tel Shalem
   :wikimedia-commons-image-page <https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Statue_of_Hadrian_from_Tel_Shalem.jpg> .
